Probably not all three. Here's the split I'd make:

**Do: a small interface plus one implementation per channel.**
```ts
interface Notifier {
  send(recipient: Recipient, message: Message): Promise<void>;
}
class EmailNotifier implements Notifier { constructor(private sendgrid: SendGridClient) {} ... }
class PushNotifier implements Notifier { ... }
class SmsNotifier implements Notifier { ... }
```
This fixes the two real problems. `OrderService` no longer builds a SendGrid client, so you can test it with a fake. Adding SMS means adding a class, not editing a switch.

**Do: a plain lookup instead of a factory.**
```ts
class OrderService {
  constructor(private notifiers: Record<Channel, Notifier>) {}
  notify(channel: Channel, ...) { return this.notifiers[channel].send(...); }
}
```
Build the map once at your composition root (wherever you wire up dependencies). A `Record<Channel, Notifier>` also makes the compiler flag a missing channel when you add a new one. A factory class only pays off if creation involves real runtime logic, such as per-tenant config or lazy construction. Nothing you've described needs that.

**Skip: the builder.** Builders help with objects that have many optional parameters or multi-step construction. Three notifiers each taking one client or config don't need one.

**Skip: a deep "strategy hierarchy".** A flat interface with three implementations is enough. Don't add abstract base classes unless the channels share real code.

**Two things to check before you start:**
- If email, push and SMS need different payloads (subject and HTML vs. title and device token vs. a 160-character body), keep `Message` generic and let each notifier format it. Don't let channel-specific fields leak into `OrderService`.
- If the switch is duplicated elsewhere, replace it everywhere in the same change.

If you later need fallbacks (SMS when push fails) or per-user channel preferences, add that as a separate layer on top of `Notifier`. You don't need to design for it now.

If you'd like, I can look at `OrderService` and sketch the refactor against your actual code.

